# Page identity and link previews

Design decision, 2026-10-18. [brief.md](brief.md) is authoritative for owner decisions; this document makes the remaining metadata choices and records assumptions. It addresses bugs [233, 240, 247, and 251](issues.md). This is a design, not a claim that the behavior is implemented or verified in production.

## Decisions

Tessel's outer document owns the tab title, favicon, canonical URL, and social tags. The embedded HTML supplies their inputs. Changing the iframe's head alone cannot update the outer document. Extract identity when an HTML revision is saved, persist the extraction as a revision-bound cache, and render metadata into the wrapper's initial server response. A scraper must not need JavaScript, iframe traversal, or a signed-in session.

Public and unlisted pages have equally rich link previews. Unlisted means excluded from discovery, not confidential: someone pasting its link into a service gives that service access to its title, description, image, and content. Private page previews always identify Tessel alone. An authorized member may see the private page's identity in their browser tab, but it must never enter social tags.

One HTML file remains sufficient. No manifest, preview upload, or new metadata form is required. Existing HTML head conventions work; missing values have automatic fallbacks.

## Visibility and audience

| Page / viewer | Browser tab and icon | Initial social metadata | Discovery | Preview image delivery |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Public, any viewer | Page subject followed by ` · Tessel`; page icon or Tessel fallback | Page-specific title, description, and image | Indexable; canonical wrapper in sitemap | Anonymous, subject to current visibility |
| Unlisted, any viewer | Same as public | Same as public | `noindex, nofollow`; absent from sitemap, feeds, search, and public listings | Anonymous when holding its opaque URL; no asset listing |
| Private, signed out or nonmember | `Tessel`; Tessel icon | Generic Tessel card only | `noindex, nofollow, noarchive`; absent from discovery | Only generic Tessel image |
| Private, authorized member | Initial `Tessel`; after authorization, page subject followed by ` · Tessel` and page icon | Generic Tessel card only, including in this member's response | Same as other private requests | Private icon requires member authorization; no private social image is generated |
| Missing, deleted, or access revoked | `Tessel`; Tessel icon | Generic Tessel card only | `noindex, nofollow, noarchive`; absent from discovery | Old page assets no longer served |

Generic means exactly title `Tessel`, description `Interactive pages on Tessel.`, Tessel's icon, and a static Tessel brand image. No page name, excerpt, author, workspace, thumbnail, private identifier in image text, or indication that the page exists. Omit page-specific canonical and `og:url` from inaccessible responses. Do not include private data in hidden markup, hydration payloads, structured data, preload tags, error messages, or response headers.

Use the same generic unavailable shell and 404 status for nonexistent pages and private pages requested without access. It can offer sign-in without confirming existence. Successful authentication returns to the requested opaque URL and membership is checked again. A known authorized member gets a normal 200 private shell. Do not grant access based on a bot user agent, link-preview service name, or referrer.

Apply robots policy in both HTML and `X-Robots-Tag`, including content and asset responses where appropriate. Do not use `robots.txt` blocking as the unlisted/private mechanism: it prevents a crawler from seeing `noindex` and provides no authorization. All embedded-content origins stay out of search; only public wrapper URLs enter the sitemap. Public-to-unlisted changes remove discovery entries but preserve rich metadata for holders of the link.

## Inputs and precedence

Parse the saved HTML with a real HTML parser, without executing scripts or fetching URLs. Read only the actual document head for head declarations. Ignore imported scripts, HTTP metadata, and declarations inserted later by scripts. For repeated declarations, use the first usable candidate in each priority tier. Keep authored candidates separate from generated results so a job never overwrites an author's declaration.

| Field | First usable value wins |
| --- | --- |
| Tab subject | `<title>`, `og:title`, `twitter:title`, first nonempty static `<h1>`, `Untitled page` |
| Social title | `og:title`, `twitter:title`, tab subject |
| Description | `<meta name="description">`, `og:description`, `twitter:description`, first meaningful static body paragraph, `An interactive page: {tab subject}.` |
| Icon | `<link rel="icon">` candidates, then `<link rel="apple-touch-icon">`, then Tessel's canonical icon |
| Preview image | Valid `og:image`, then `twitter:image`, then automatic generation described below |

Append ` · Tessel` to tab and social subjects once, avoiding duplication of that exact trailing suffix. Social title normally matches the tab title; an explicitly authored social title may differ. Do not let user-supplied canonical, `og:url`, or `og:site_name` override Tessel's wrapper URL or site identity.

Decode entities, collapse whitespace, remove control characters, and escape all output as text or attribute values. Cap extracted subjects at 160 characters and descriptions at 300, cutting at a word boundary. These limits bound display output; never rewrite the source HTML. Ignore script, style, template, navigation, hidden, and form-control text when extracting a body fallback. A JavaScript-only body can use the subject-based description and still has a complete card.

Relative asset references resolve against the saved document's content URL, never the wrapper URL or the save API URL. Ignore an authored `<base>` for extraction; this deliberately makes metadata resolution predictable and prevents it redirecting asset fetches. Support same-page assets and bounded image data URLs so a single self-contained HTML file works. External assets must use HTTPS. Reject script schemes, local filesystem URLs, blob URLs, and credential-bearing URLs. Failed candidates fall through rather than failing publication.

For icons, prefer a valid scalable icon, then the largest suitable square raster candidate; emit a sanitized SVG when available and PNG/ICO fallbacks derived from the same selected source. Treat uploaded SVG as untrusted: remove active/external content or rasterize it in isolation. Generate a 180×180 touch icon as well. The outer head must have one deliberate icon set; remove conflicting inherited defaults. Tessel toolbar branding remains Tessel's identity.

An authenticated private viewer receives page title/icon data only through the authorized page-data path, never public extraction endpoints. Use the existing authorized load rather than a second title request. Reset the outer title and icon immediately on navigation, sign-out, access loss, or switching pages, before the next page loads. Tear down the previous iframe; recheck access when restoring a suspended tab. Verify router head updates cannot overwrite the resolved identity. Dynamic iframe title changes are not promoted in this version; save a new HTML revision to change identity.

## Initial wrapper response

For accessible public/unlisted pages, emit the following in the initial head, using stored revision metadata and absolute, stage-correct HTTPS URLs on trusted configured origins:

- `<title>`, description, icon links, and the canonical wrapper URL.
- `og:type=website`, `og:site_name=Tessel`, `og:title`, `og:description`, `og:url`, `og:image`, `og:image:type`, image width/height, and `og:image:alt`.
- `twitter:card=summary_large_image`, `twitter:title`, `twitter:description`, `twitter:image`, and `twitter:image:alt`.
- The appropriate robots directive. Tracking query parameters and fragments do not change identity or canonical URL.

Use the same complete metadata resolver for full loads and client navigation; do not allow a nested route override to drop image or card fields. Never reflect an arbitrary Host header into generated URLs. Reuse the existing stable page URL; if new URL identities are needed, use opaque IDs without titles so a later private transition cannot leave a sensitive name in the path. This design does not change production domains.

For private pages, render only the generic metadata even after authorization. Only the member's tab title/icon change after the authorized page load. For public discovery, expose escaped static heading/description and meaningful static text/links in the wrapper's no-JavaScript presentation, without executing user HTML on Tessel's origin. A JavaScript-only page at least exposes its extracted title and description; this design does not promise full indexing of arbitrary interactive content.

## Preview images

The output is a 1200×630 PNG, served through a Tessel-controlled asset endpoint with matching MIME type and dimensions. It is never a live iframe or a URL that asks a third party to render user code.

1. **Declared image:** validate and copy the first usable authored preview image into controlled storage. Preserve its composition inside the card frame without stretching or silently cropping important content; pad when the aspect ratio differs. Use the matching declared image alt text, otherwise `Preview of {social subject}`. Do not require a screenshot when the author already supplied a valid image.
2. **Automatic image:** create a deterministic Tessel card immediately from the subject, description, canonical product mark, and product colors/type. This is page-specific and always available, including while richer generation is pending. Generate a screenshot of the saved revision asynchronously, allowing bounded JavaScript execution so client-built pages can appear. On success, place the screenshot inside the same Tessel card frame with the subject and product mark. Use `Preview of {social subject}` as alt text. Browser scripts cannot replace stored titles or descriptions.
3. **Failure:** if execution times out, assets fail, or the screenshot is empty, retain the deterministic subject card. Never serve a blank card, a spinner, an error screenshot, or a previous revision's image as though it were current.

Screenshot the page content, not the toolbar, member cursor, editor UI, login prompt, or a particular viewer's state. Use a fresh desktop viewport, light color preference, reduced motion, and no stored browser state; freeze animations after the bounded capture window. Initial limits: 5 seconds for content readiness, 10 seconds total execution, 20 MB aggregate asset traffic, and 100 resource requests. These are tunable implementation defaults, not promises to authors. A page needing unavailable services gets the deterministic card.

Public and unlisted revisions may be rendered. Do not screenshot private pages or retain a private social image for future publication. When a private page becomes public/unlisted, generate from the then-current published revision. While it is private, an authorized editor's preview shows the generic card that others will receive.

## Preview execution must not act

Bug 251 is an isolation failure. A renderer must be unable to cast votes or perform other mutations even if the HTML does so on load. A `preview=true` query parameter, simulated user identity, or JavaScript flag is not a security boundary.

- Render the saved revision in a disposable sandbox, with no user cookies, sessions, API keys, auth headers, local storage, service workers, or ambient cloud credentials. Supply the input snapshot before execution; never navigate an authenticated production page to capture it.
- Do not load the writable Tessel runtime. Supply a compatible preview runtime whose reads return a frozen, explicitly public snapshot (or empty defaults) and whose writes always fail with `PREVIEW_READ_ONLY`. Never mint a viewer write capability. No private/member data enters this snapshot. Check permission again at every production mutation boundary; renderer identity has no write capability.
- Deny all direct outbound browser network access. Supply approved static asset bytes through an isolated fetch broker; block runtime APIs, navigation, forms, beacons, WebSockets, service-worker registration, downloads, popups, and nested frames. Blocking POST alone is insufficient: a GET URL can also cause a side effect. Do not forward arbitrary GET requests made by page JavaScript. Preview-safe reads use the snapshot interface, not live endpoints.
- To avoid treating an arbitrary URL as a harmless image, automatic screenshots use only revision-owned assets and already ingested metadata assets. External dependencies require explicit ingestion into the revision's static asset set; arbitrary remote scripts, live APIs, and tracking pixels are unavailable during rendering. This trades screenshot fidelity for predictable isolation; the subject card covers failures.
- The asset broker handles authored external icon/preview-image imports once at save time, without credentials or referrers, with HTTPS-only requests, no internal/loopback/link-local/metadata addresses, DNS pinning and redirect revalidation. Limit redirects to 3, each fetch to 5 seconds and 5 MB, and decoded images to 20 megapixels; validate actual bytes before decoding in a sandbox. No renderer can invoke this broker on arbitrary URLs. An author-provided external image URL necessarily receives an import request; it is not fetched on every paste.
- Jobs never increment votes, views, analytics, activity, or billing counters through the page runtime. Do not count asset or metadata requests as visits. Apply CPU/memory/network bounds and concurrency limits to jobs; recycle the sandbox after each capture.

The guarantee covers Tessel's generation path and ordinary metadata scrapers. Tessel cannot prevent an unrelated third-party crawler from loading an anonymously accessible public page like a browser. Therefore GET/HEAD in Tessel's own data API never mutate state, and default runtime mutations require an explicit interaction and a server-issued, narrowly scoped viewer capability. Any anonymous background-write capability needs a separate product decision; it is not introduced here. Do not claim to prove a human gesture from a client boolean.

## Persistence, freshness, and revocation

The database owns published revision, visibility, membership, metadata selection, and job state; object storage holds image/icon bytes in a nonpublic bucket. Extracted fields and screenshots are disposable caches keyed by revision, metadata algorithm version, and visibility generation. Preserve authored inputs in the HTML. Asset keys contain no page title, filename, or identifying text.

On publish/save, parse synchronously and make the current revision's deterministic card available. Validate declared images and produce screenshots through deduplicated jobs, never in response to a crawler request. One job per revision/generation may be active. Record `pending`, `ready`, or `failed` internally; editors see a card plus `Updating preview` or `Couldn't update preview` and a retry action. Publishing and reading remain available when image processing fails. Expensive rendering retries only on explicit editor retry or a new revision, not every request.

Jobs capture page ID, owner, revision, and visibility generation. Recheck owner liveness, page existence, current revision, and visibility before reading inputs and before publishing output. A superseded job cannot attach its result. Register object writes before uploading so orphan cleanup can find them even if publication or deletion races the upload. Delete obsolete artifacts through bounded resumable cleanup after they stop being deliverable.

Every HTML, metadata, icon, and image request checks current visibility/access before serving bytes or returning a conditional 304. Revision URLs bust normal image caches but never bypass current authorization. Use opaque asset URLs mapped through the database; never redirect to an unrestricted bucket URL or issue a long-lived signed URL for content-derived preview assets.

Initially serve wrapper HTML and page-derived assets with `Cache-Control: no-store` and disable CDN caching of their response bodies. A serving edge can rate-limit and route requests, but must not return previously cached content without the current access check. Static generic Tessel assets can be cached indefinitely under versioned URLs. Render caches in storage avoid expensive regeneration without being public delivery caches. Consider faster shared caching only with a separately verified revocation design.

On visibility change, deletion, or revocation, update policy and advance the generation atomically, invalidate app caches, cancel pending work, and stop serving formerly accessible assets before acknowledging completion. Existing authorized tabs clear identity/content on the next access event; push access changes to connected clients. All requests beginning after the change commits must apply the new policy. Bytes already delivered or in an in-flight response cannot be recalled.

Turning public/unlisted into private replaces every social field with the generic card and denies old image/icon URLs to anonymous callers. Public-to-unlisted also removes discovery entries and sets `noindex`; a search engine may take time to revisit it. Tessel cannot erase screenshots, downloaded HTML, browser history, or chat cards cached by third parties. State in the sharing UI: `Previously shared previews may remain in other services.` Offer refresh instructions where services support them; never promise retroactive secrecy.

## Author experience and examples

The sharing area shows the actual anonymous card and the visibility explanation. Public: `Anyone can open this page, and it can appear in search.` Unlisted: `Anyone with the link can open this page and see its preview. It won't appear in Tessel discovery.` Private: `Only invited members can open this page. Shared links show a generic Tessel preview.` No new authoring step blocks publication.

This single file is sufficient for a custom identity:

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <title>Friday lunch poll</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Pick where we eat this Friday.">
    <link rel="icon" href="data:image/png;base64,...">
    <!-- Optional; omit it to get an automatic preview image. -->
    <meta property="og:image" content="https://assets.example/Friday.png">
  </head>
  <body><!-- The interactive page, including inline JavaScript. --></body>
</html>
```

For public/unlisted, its tab is `Friday lunch poll · Tessel`, its card uses that title and the authored description, and it prefers the declared image if validation succeeds. Without that image, it gets an automatic card. Without any head metadata, static heading/paragraph extraction or the `Untitled page` fallback keeps publishing functional. For a private `Q3 layoffs plan`, a member's tab may show `Q3 layoffs plan · Tessel`; every social card shows only `Tessel` and the static brand image.

## Acceptance criteria and bug coverage

- **233:** Request the private layoffs page signed out, as a nonmember, and as a known scraper. Search the complete HTML, headers, payloads, and image bytes for its title/paragraph; none may appear. An authorized response still has generic social tags. Anonymous access to old image/icon URLs after a public-to-private change is denied. Missing and inaccessible pages have the same response shape.
- **240:** Publish an HTML file with title and favicon, then verify the outer browser tab and touch icon. Test reload, client navigation between pages, fallback icons, malformed assets, router head changes, private member access, sign-out, and permission loss. The prior page's identity must not persist while another page loads.
- **247:** Publish a page that constructs its body only with JavaScript. A plain HTTP fetch must contain a full page-specific OG/large-image card immediately, including a reachable fallback image while capture is pending. Successful isolated capture upgrades the image; failed capture leaves a useful subject card. Verify public and unlisted cards by pasting signed-out links into representative chat/social tools and exercising their refresh controls.
- **251:** Use a test page that tries runtime writes, fetch POST and GET mutations, beacons, socket traffic, hidden forms, and on-load voting. Publish, generate, retry, and paste the URL repeatedly. Assert zero vote/data/visit changes from Tessel's preview path and no outbound bypass. Test denial at the server capability boundary as well as in the renderer.
- Check all visibility/viewer combinations, source precedence, inline images/icons, unsafe URLs/SVGs, script-only fallbacks, complete metadata after navigation, robots/canonical/sitemap behavior, and stage-correct absolute URLs. Verify no JavaScript client can fetch private extraction data without membership.
- Race a running job with edits, deletion, and visibility changes. Old jobs must not publish; previously issued asset URLs and conditional requests must recheck access. Confirm pending/failed images never break publication and retries do not duplicate active jobs.

## Assumptions made while the owner is away

- The brief's unlisted access permits disclosure to a service that receives its link. It does not imply anonymous viewers are trusted or that `noindex` provides secrecy.
- There is one published HTML revision to preview. Unsaved drafts, personalized runtime data, and a viewer's current interaction state are excluded. If drafts become shareable, they need their own explicit access contract.
- Existing standard head tags are the authoring interface. Dynamic document-head mutations are intentionally excluded from persisted metadata; no custom manifest or metadata editor is added.
- A member may see private identity in their own tab after authorization. Social metadata remains generic even for members. This resolves the private leak without making all member tabs indistinguishable.
- Declared preview images outrank automatic generation. A branded subject card is a valid automatic preview, and safe screenshots are an enhancement. Arbitrary external dependency execution is not required for publication or preview availability.
- Existing runtime writes can be separated from preview reads and ordinary page GETs. That separation is a prerequisite to screenshots; until it is enforced, ship declared/subject images and leave executable capture disabled.
- Numeric limits, the separator, generic copy, and light desktop capture are initial design choices. No domain, provider, billing, deployment, or auth configuration is changed by this document.

These decisions can be implemented without waiting for the owner. Revisit dynamic tab-title support, custom metadata controls, and richer remote dependency capture only if later product needs justify them; none blocks these four bugs.
